# Policies

**Written by people. `/specsmith:init` never overwrites this file.**

Conventions describe how this codebase is written. Policies describe what the
organization requires of it — security, data handling, operations. They are not
inferred from the code, because the code is exactly what may be violating them.

Owners: <who reviews changes to this file — security, architecture>
Last reviewed: <date>

---

## Enabled profiles

Regulatory profiles shipped with SpecSmith. Nothing is enabled by default. Enable a
profile by listing it here; its rules are then applied to every spec in this repository.

```yaml
profiles:
  # - ru/152-fz-pdn        # personal data (152-ФЗ)
```

Available: see `policies/` in the SpecSmith repository. Only public regulations ship
with the plugin.

---

## Local rules

Rules specific to this organization. Same triad as a profile rule — a trigger the spec
phase can match, a requirement, and what the spec must therefore contain. A rule without
a trigger never fires; a rule without "what must be in the spec" produces nothing an
author can act on.

### LOCAL-01 · <title>

**Trigger:** <what in a spec makes this rule apply>

**Requirement:** <what must hold>

**Must appear in the spec:** <the specific facts the spec has to state>

**Owner:** <team> · **Reviewed:** <date>

---

## How these are applied

SpecSmith matches triggers against a finished spec and lists the policies it touches. It
**routes to a reviewer — it does not certify compliance.** A spec that names every
policy it touches has not been approved by anyone; it is ready to be shown to the people
who approve things.
